1
00:00:02,640 --> 00:00:08,480
The following is a production of Frank Defratus and the Internet Museum of Holography.

2
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:20,800
The date is November 2098, 100 years into the future.

3
00:00:20,800 --> 00:00:29,360
The scene is a memorabilia show dealing with antiques and collectibles from the late 20th century.

4
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,480
Hello, this is Frank Defratis, and welcome to another week of HoloTalk.

5
00:00:34,480 --> 00:00:45,120
On this special broadcast, we're going to be taking a look at something I term antiquarian holographica, which is the collection of holographic memorabilia.

6
00:00:45,120 --> 00:00:59,760
At our show, we see rare and first edition books, period maps and lithographs, and in a corner at the end of a long aisle is a booth dealing exclusively with holography.

7
00:00:59,760 --> 00:01:14,000
Our imaginary booth has quite a selection, embossed holograms, including several limited edition pieces produced back in the late 1980s and 90s by a number of prominent artists.

8
00:01:14,000 --> 00:01:20,080
A copy of the famous National Geographic cover with the American Eagle.

9
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:36,080
Signatures and autographs of the early movers and shakers in the field, posters and programs from early international shows, and first edition copies of many out-of-print books on holography.

10
00:01:36,080 --> 00:01:45,360
And there's a real attention getter as well, an actual helium neon laser that the person in the booth tells you really works.

11
00:01:45,360 --> 00:01:50,760
That is, if you can find a place that still uses electricity for power.

12
00:01:50,400 --> 00:01:53,880
Our little scenario is not that far-fetched.

13
00:01:54,120 --> 00:02:08,280
Many of the items related to holography that we pass over as insignificant, stuffed into our desk drawers and filing cabinets, will someday be looked upon as period pieces worthy of collection.

14
00:02:08,280 --> 00:02:27,320
And since we can be fairly certain that we won't be around to attend our little imaginary convention in 2098, why not make the most of the time that we do have by attempting to put together an impressive collection of future holographic memorabilia?

15
00:02:27,320 --> 00:02:38,920
Holotalk receives quite a few emails on a regular basis from its listeners, and many of those emails are asking for specific information relating to collecting.

16
00:02:38,920 --> 00:02:47,400
One person wrote asking for information concerning a specific embossed hologram and how to acquire one for their collection.

17
00:02:47,400 --> 00:03:00,680
Another from Europe spoke of the writer's pride in the collection of books and other printed materials, all related to the field of holography, but added that they were concerned that it was not complete.

18
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:08,520
Could it be, I wondered, that collecting holographic-related items was catching on around the globe?

19
00:03:08,520 --> 00:03:12,920
The answer to that question appears to be in the affirmative.

20
00:03:12,920 --> 00:03:22,520
Yes, there is collecting going on, and the real surprise is that it may prove to be the most active aspect of the field.

21
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:27,320
There appears to be a fever that goes hand in hand with the art of collecting.

22
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:40,040
Collectors can sometimes go through extreme trouble searching out materials, and nowhere are those materials more new and exciting than holography.

23
00:03:40,040 --> 00:03:55,600
For a field celebrating its first few decades of existence, there is a multitude of collecting possibilities, including tracking down books and related articles that extend much further back in time than just a few decades.

24
00:03:55,600 --> 00:04:12,000
There is a rich history and many centuries of art and science behind it, some of it extending back into the 16th and 17th centuries, all of it relating to holography today.

25
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:34,560
Searching out the articles, books, posters, and holograms from around the world to add to one's personal collection of holographic future memorabilia.

26
00:04:34,560 --> 00:05:00,080
The mission of this broadcast is to provide a solid foundation for starting one's own archive on holography for future generations who will look back in time not only through the eyes of a collector, but a keeper of antiquarian holographica.

27
00:05:00,080 --> 00:05:08,400
As with any new endeavor, it is important to have a plan, an ultimate destination for your collection.

28
00:05:08,400 --> 00:05:13,040
Will you concentrate on books and publications?

29
00:05:13,040 --> 00:05:20,480
Embossed holograms, posters and commemorative of exhibits, all of the above and more?

30
00:05:20,480 --> 00:05:28,720
Remember, there is little, if any, background material available concerning collecting holography.

31
00:05:28,720 --> 00:05:39,560
So much of the groundwork, legwork, and penwork, along with many hours of research, will have to be done by you, the collector.

32
00:05:39,560 --> 00:05:50,120
The more you know, the more your collection will benefit, since much of your knowledge will show through in your aesthetic judgments and decisions.

33
00:05:50,120 --> 00:05:57,480
Whatever your plan is for reaching your goal, it should never be executed in a haphazard way.

34
00:05:57,480 --> 00:06:06,040
Decisions should be made intelligently and with great care as to what is worth having the honor of being part of your collection.

35
00:06:06,040 --> 00:06:17,240
Remember, this isn't clutter or garage sale mentality we're talking about here, but a systematic recording of the history of holography.

36
00:06:17,480 --> 00:06:23,000
Let's take a look at what a typical collection might contain.

37
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:27,880
We'll start with holograms.

38
00:06:27,880 --> 00:06:35,960
Embossed holograms would be represented from around the world, both stock images and images that are unique.

39
00:06:54,360 --> 00:07:03,560
There are also magazines, books, catalogs, brochures that have holograms on their cover or inside the issue.

40
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:16,960
These printed pieces and the embossed holograms would be carefully placed in black sleeves, numbered, identified, and stored carefully away from thumbing curiosity seekers.

41
00:07:16,960 --> 00:07:25,440
Complete sets, such as the ET stickers from Hershey Reese's pieces, would round out the collection.

42
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:32,000
A unique ET set would have all nine images on one untouched sheet of foil.

43
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:38,320
These are much harder to come by, so would be valued more for a collection.

44
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:42,800
The new photopolymer holograms would also be considered a collectible.

45
00:07:42,800 --> 00:07:53,120
The reflection holograms produced on film and sold throughout retail outlets across the US are also a consideration.

46
00:07:53,120 --> 00:07:55,680
Publications.

47
00:08:46,880 --> 00:08:49,680
Books.

48
00:08:49,680 --> 00:08:57,840
Many books would be represented dealing with holography and its branches of lasers, light, and even philosophy.

49
00:08:57,840 --> 00:08:59,440
One thing is certain.

50
00:08:59,720 --> 00:09:03,240
Books are an expensive part of collecting.

51
00:09:03,240 --> 00:09:12,920
Most books dealing with holography are not old enough or published in large enough numbers to be easily found on the used book rack.

52
00:09:12,920 --> 00:09:18,600
Holography is a technical science, and technical manuals can run up quite a bill.

53
00:09:18,600 --> 00:09:26,920
Fortunately, there are enough over-the-counter inexpensive soft covers to keep a collector busy for a long time.

54
00:09:26,920 --> 00:09:36,120
Most bookstores will gladly special order titles for you, although some may put an additional finder's fee on the final cost.

55
00:09:36,120 --> 00:09:39,960
But these charges are usually minimal.

56
00:09:39,960 --> 00:09:48,040
First editions, especially those that are out of print, are a collector's dream and a field unto itself.

57
00:09:48,040 --> 00:09:51,800
This is the creme de la creme of book collecting.

58
00:09:51,800 --> 00:09:58,920
Whether you need first editions or simply a well-handled later edition is a matter of preference.

59
00:10:03,640 --> 00:10:11,080
So looking to the future is a consideration that must be eventually confronted.

60
00:10:11,080 --> 00:10:14,520
Letters and autographs.

61
00:10:14,520 --> 00:10:19,000
Letters and autographs are collectibles too.

62
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:26,120
Letters are more of a personal collectible since many shouldn't be put on display or passed around.

63
00:10:26,120 --> 00:10:33,640
A letter or document written entirely in the hand of its author is called a holograph.

64
00:10:33,640 --> 00:10:38,200
A standalone signature is an autograph.

65
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:47,760
The letters I have on file are obviously due to the HoloTalk program and a newsletter that I published back in the eighties.

66
00:10:48,640 --> 00:10:55,680
But if you were to write to a holographer, I'm sure they would be glad to write a short note back.

67
00:10:55,680 --> 00:11:05,280
Since I don't know of any holographers who currently need to have protection from crowds of admirers, I'm sure your request would be flattering.

68
00:11:05,280 --> 00:11:11,200
Remember, use discretion displaying the letter, note card, or autograph.

69
00:11:11,200 --> 00:11:14,800
And don't pass over the beauty of the envelope.

70
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:20,240
Many foreign stamps, as well as US are truly works of art.

71
00:11:20,240 --> 00:11:31,440
The quality and feel of the paper, the design and printing of the letterhead, the penmanship get a feel for all these things.

72
00:11:34,400 --> 00:11:43,840
Many posters, announcements, and catalogs are still available through holography museums, galleries and manufacturers.

73
00:11:43,840 --> 00:11:54,240
Many years ago, through neglect, I lost an original poster for the inaugural tour of the New York Museum of Hologies through the looking glass.

74
00:11:54,240 --> 00:11:57,040
Don't make the same mistake.

75
00:11:57,040 --> 00:12:01,600
Either put it on the wall or keep it in a cylinder.

76
00:12:01,600 --> 00:12:05,120
Equipment catalogs are highly collectible.

77
00:12:05,120 --> 00:12:13,360
Not only do they show photos of the error's existing technology, but many give technical drawings also.

78
00:12:13,360 --> 00:12:19,600
Newport research catalogs have photos and blueprints for every piece of equipment.

79
00:12:19,600 --> 00:12:35,000
As the field progresses, and mirrors, lenses, and filters are gradually replaced by fiber optic and holographic optical elements, and whatever will come after that, these catalogs will take on an air of nostalgia.

80
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:38,680
Just call a company and ask for a catalog.

81
00:12:38,680 --> 00:12:47,560
Tell them your 300 plus employee company grossed 150 million last year, and you'll get it overnight express.

82
00:12:47,560 --> 00:12:49,400
How about patents?

83
00:12:49,400 --> 00:12:55,320
Well, the way things are going, the patents may become more famous than many holograms.

84
00:12:55,320 --> 00:12:58,600
You can call the patent office in Washington, D.C.

85
00:12:58,760 --> 00:13:06,040
and receive for a nominal fee all the patents applied for and granted in the field of holography.

86
00:13:06,040 --> 00:13:10,520
Patents make a great addition to any collection.

87
00:13:10,520 --> 00:13:15,560
Overall, collecting should be an enjoyable and rewarding experience.

88
00:13:15,560 --> 00:13:20,360
You may have a collection started already and just never looked at it as so.

89
00:13:20,360 --> 00:13:26,760
Go through your drawers and file cabinets, bookshelves, etc., and begin sorting things out.

90
00:13:26,760 --> 00:13:29,640
You may be surprised at what's there.

91
00:13:29,640 --> 00:13:36,120
Keep an eye open at all times for holograms and articles, books and magazines.

92
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:55,960
With the interest in holography continuing at an ever accelerating pace, and individuals who began their interest in holography several years ago now becoming more sophisticated in their appreciation of the medium, the time is right for a blossoming of interest in collecting works of holography.

93
00:13:55,960 --> 00:14:09,800
However, while the most prominent artists in the field are beginning to find clientele willing to make substantial investments in their work, the majority of these works are priced beyond the means of the average enthusiast.

94
00:14:09,800 --> 00:14:14,920
This leaves an area completely void, which is waiting to participate in collecting holograms.

95
00:14:16,800 --> 00:14:35,280
It is believed that we may begin to fill that void by suggesting that a would-be collector begin by concentrating his or her efforts on building a collection of low cost and in many cases no cost, readily available embossed holograms.

96
00:14:35,280 --> 00:14:46,480
Now, one may immediately remark that if embossed holograms are low cost and readily available, what would the collecting interest be?

97
00:14:46,480 --> 00:14:56,480
To answer this, it must be pointed out that although many embossed holograms are low in cost, there are many that are difficult to find.

98
00:14:56,480 --> 00:15:15,520
Also, since several holographic artists have created works that were limited edition embossed holograms, it is possible to own a work with only a modest investment by such artists as Harriett Casden Silver, John Kaufman, Dan Schweitzer, among others.

99
00:15:15,520 --> 00:15:21,920
There are a few embossed holograms that are becoming harder and harder to find.

100
00:15:24,720 --> 00:15:34,320
However, once located, an older, out-of-print, limited run embossed hologram is a treasure to own and view.

101
00:15:34,320 --> 00:15:37,760
It is truly a collector's piece.

102
00:15:37,760 --> 00:15:41,440
What does a beginning collector need to know?

103
00:15:41,680 --> 00:15:44,240
Let's break it down.

104
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:51,960
It would be possible to have a combination of any single group classification with any single subgroup.

105
00:15:50,480 --> 00:15:55,240
Let's clarify and define each group and subgroup.

106
00:15:56,720 --> 00:16:03,320
Our groups are group one, two D graphic stickers.

107
00:16:03,320 --> 00:16:11,960
These are the images that are line drawings or graphics and are essentially flat surface pictures.

108
00:16:11,960 --> 00:16:19,560
Usually another 2D flat surface image will appear a short depth away from the front image.

109
00:16:19,560 --> 00:16:23,560
The second image was usually a neutral field.

110
00:16:23,560 --> 00:16:27,640
Many holograms of this type used a background of stars.

111
00:16:27,640 --> 00:16:36,280
A good example for collection purposes would be the famous ET stickers found in packages of Reese's pieces.

112
00:16:36,280 --> 00:16:40,680
The second group would be 3D images.

113
00:16:40,680 --> 00:16:54,840
Now these embossed images are the same as a regular rainbow or white light transmission hologram that uses a three-dimensional object, be it real or abstract, as its image.

114
00:16:54,840 --> 00:17:00,680
A good example would be one of the National Geographic covers.

115
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:11,480
Subgroups, now these groups are the subgroups of either group one, which is the 2D graphic sticker, or group two, 3D images.

116
00:17:24,120 --> 00:17:30,360
An example would be any retail hologram sold only for its own purpose.

117
00:17:30,360 --> 00:17:35,000
Subgroup B, packaging holograms.

118
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:51,840
Embossed holograms included in the packaging design of a consumer related product or its packaging materials, usually designed to attract or call attention to the product and enhance the product's visibility and or appeal.

119
00:17:51,840 --> 00:17:59,520
Also used by companies to promote itself as future oriented or having a forward looking vision.

120
00:17:59,520 --> 00:18:06,160
An example would be the hologram on a box for a camera.

121
00:18:06,160 --> 00:18:11,840
Subgroup C artistic holograms.

122
00:18:11,840 --> 00:18:29,360
These are usually limited edition pieces created by an established holographic artist with the masters made in the artist studio lab or rental facility and then mass produced by embossing, similar to a lithograph made from a painting.

123
00:18:29,360 --> 00:18:44,560
An example would be Harriet Casden's Silver Magic Teeth, which had a run of 1,750 pieces and was offered in a sleeve that contained the artist's signature.

124
00:18:44,560 --> 00:18:51,120
Subgroup D, experimental prototype holograms.

125
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:59,600
This type of embossed hologram is valued as a collectible for the simple reason that something about it makes it one of a kind.

126
00:18:59,600 --> 00:19:14,160
An embossed hologram that was an artist's proof and still contained crop marks and cut lines would make that piece unique from the others that were to follow for retail sale.

127
00:19:14,160 --> 00:19:22,760
While a person can certainly become overwhelmed by the sheer number of embossed holograms that have flooded the market since their inception.

128
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:31,960
With a certain amount of skill and work, it can become possible to build quite an impressive collection of embossed holographic images.

129
00:19:32,920 --> 00:19:44,520
With regards to acceptance of embossed holograms within the holographic community, a purist attitude once prevailed in the collective minds of holographic artists.

130
00:19:44,520 --> 00:19:59,880
Their initial concern about the viewing of their work, now removed from the hermetically sealed environment of the holography galleries and museums and now out on the street with the general public, was soon found to ring true.

131
00:20:07,880 --> 00:20:13,400
At the site of the smeared gray image of the National Geographic cover.

132
00:20:41,160 --> 00:20:49,160
Since an only added to rather than subtracted from holography's somewhat mystical appeal.

133
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:07,600
And you will have a hologram that will stand up visually to anything produced in limited quantities on glass plates.

134
00:21:07,600 --> 00:21:15,280
Only a discerning eye, or someone with knowledge of the holographic process itself, would find discrepancies.

135
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:27,920
Embossed holograms are beautiful and fun to collect, and for a modest investment of your time and money, you can build an impressive collection of holograms for display.
